{
  "result": "The morning arrives without asking permission. It seeps through the curtains in thin, patient lines, finding the dust that the night has settled on the bookshelf, on the mug left from yesterday, on the soft curve of a sleeping cat. I make tea because the ritual is easier than thinking, and because steam rising from a cup gives the room something to do while I remember who I am supposed to become today. Outside, a bicycle bell rings twice, cheerful and unhurried. Someone is already late for something important, or early for something unimportant, and either way they are singing to the street.\n\nI think about all the versions of a life people carry quietly in their pockets. The librarian who once wanted to sail around the world. The bus driver who writes poems on receipts when traffic stalls. The teenager who practices piano with headphones on, so the neighbors will not hear the mistakes. We are not defined by the grand gesture we imagine at midnight, but by the small repetitions we forgive ourselves for in daylight. A spilled coffee does not ruin a morning. A forgotten name does not ruin a friendship. A rainy Tuesday does not ruin a season. This is not optimism, exactly. It is more like housekeeping for the spirit.\n\nLater, I will walk to the market, buy too many oranges, and talk to the old woman who always asks if I have eaten, because love often sounds like lunch. That is enough for today.",
